* Semester Work in the Age of AI

- Companies will demand the use of AI tools such as SWE-Agents
- A written semester work at home has become too easy with AI tools
- You may use AI tools, but you have to disclose how you used them
- You are responsible for everything you hand in and must be able to explain it
- ⮕ More weight on the presentation and the questions afterwards
- ⮕ More weight on *explaining*: How do you teach a concept to your fellow students?
- ⮕ *Peer* *reviews*: You learn from giving and receiving feedback

* Semester Work

- *Presentation* of a topic, followed by questions
- Short written paper with code examples
- Quiz for your fellow students
- *Peer* *reviews* of other presentations

All details: Learning Campus

* Semester Work: Topics

Choose one of:

- A *concept* (e.g. generics, coroutines, ownership, pattern matching)
- A *programming* *language* with a special concept (e.g. Zig, Elixir, Julia, Forth, APL)
- A *theoretical* topic
- A topic *off* *the* *beaten* *track* (e.g. "Why is JavaScript so fast?", "What happens before main()?")

Suggestions: ⮕ Miro Board

